                                    problem.</b></span></p><p class="mb-10" href="" id="cc-id-bYqENCiX0fpz"><b id="cc-id-4KfwvX5kZ7Ru" class="rte-styled-text">They're an enzyme problem.</b></p><p class="mb-10" href="" id="cc-id-IDyEmKzAx6w2">
                                Deep inside your skin, there's an enzyme called
                                <i id="cc-id-luhxHE3MK4kf" class="rte-styled-text">tyrosinase.</i></p><p class="mb-10" href="" id="cc-id-qmp1JzbMjFqP">
                                Think of it like a pipe.
                              </p><p class="mb-10" href="" id="cc-id-EPKIsXKrZAiH">
                                In young skin, it drips normally — producing
                                just enough melanin to give your skin its
                                natural color.
                              </p><p class="mb-10" href="" id="cc-id-SUdghLllseZD">
                                But after years of sun exposure, hormonal
                                shifts, and inflammation, that pipe starts
                                leaking.
                              </p><p class="mb-10" href="" id="cc-id-5Tfx0fpIRS97">
                                Melanin floods the skin. Pools in clusters.
                                Rises to the surface. And what you see in the
